For more options for runners, see our Best Men’s Road Running Shoes guide.

Why you should buy walking shoes

Shoes designed for walking differ from other running shoes or hiking shoes in that they’re crafted for the heel-to-toe striking motion of walking. They typically offer cushion to absorb impact on the joints and support underfoot to help reduce the risk of injuries. The cushion and support of walking shoes are also meant to help keep you comfortable during long days on your feet, whether while working, commuting, traveling, or walking your dog.

A quality pair of walking shoes should help you maintain a proper gait, encouraging forward motion only slightly, unlike running-specific shoes that may roll and propel you forward more. They should have just enough responsiveness to help you feel more energized and peppy while you’re walking without making you feel like you should be running. However, you can walk in running shoes, which is particularly useful if you’re walking for exercise, though this might encourage more forward motion than you need for more casual walking and everyday wear.

A walking shoe with proper fit, adequate arch support, and enough protective cushioning can help set you up for long-term foot health success. The right walking shoe can help prevent injuries by reducing impact on your joints, correcting your gait, and making you feel generally more comfortable as you move.

If walking shoes sound like just another reason someone’s telling you to spend money, consider them more of an investment in your health. For example, the author of this guide spent four years as a high school teacher and cross country/track coach, which included at least 10 hours on her feet per day. She was usually training for marathons on top of that, which meant even more time on her feet. At the time, she often wore unsupportive flats while teaching and eventually developed super gnarly plantar fasciitis in both feet, which took about 9 months to heal and included a lot of expensive doctor appointments. A quality pair of walking shoes would have been cheaper (and much less painful and frustrating).

If you walk a lot to work while commuting, or walk a lot during work, a supportive, comfortable walking shoe that is stylish is also worth investing in.

However, if you already have running shoes (that aren’t old and worn down), and don’t want to spend money on yet another pair of shoes, that’s totally OK. Most running shoes work well as walking shoes, and if you’ll wear them, that’s great! Once you are in the market for a new pair of shoes, you can consider walking-specific shoes, or even active recovery shoes that work for both walking/running.

What to look for in walking shoes

Arch support

Walking shoes should offer enough support to prevent foot fatigue or pain. In particular, arch support should be a priority when selecting a walking shoe. However, since not everyone’s arches are the same, first you’ll want to figure out if you have high arches, average arches, or flat feet.

There’s a simple way you can determine this at home by wetting your feet and stepping on a piece of paper. If you see about half your arch in the wet outline, you likely have average arches. If the arches are more filled in, you may have low arches or flat feet, and if the arches are not filled in, you may have high arches. Some local running stores can measure your feet, and tell you your arch height. There are even more options to consider, like getting a 3D foot scan, if you have extremely high or extremely flat arches, have struggled with foot pain in the past, or if you have uneven feet.

Most of the shoes we’ve tested work well for average arches. If you have high arches, you may want to consider neutral shoes with more cushion, like the HOKA Bondi SR, HOKA Bondi 9, or other cushioned road running shoes. For those with low arches or flat feet, you may want a stability or motion-control shoe, like the HOKA Arahi SR, or neutral shoes with custom inserts.

If you’re interested in zero-drop or barefoot shoes, a good rule of thumb is to adjust your feet slowly to them. You can do this by gradually swapping the barefoot shoes into your routine for growing increments of time. This helps prevent injuries that can occur if you switch over too quickly and don’t have the adequate foot strength.

Foot width

When choosing a walking shoe, consider whether your feet are narrow, average, or wide. You can measure your foot width at home by standing on a piece of paper, outlining your foot, and measuring the width at the widest part.

We recommend taking this measurement in the afternoon or after you’ve been on your feet for a long time, since feet can swell and you’ll want to know your shoes will accommodate that swelling. Once you have your width measurement, you can compare it to brand sizing charts like New Balance’s shoe width chart.

Cushioning and midsole technology

Comfort is the top priority in a walking shoe because if you aren’t comfortable, you won’t wear them. Midsole cushion plays a major role in your perceived comfort. Choose a pair with the right amount of cushion for you.

Higher-cushion walking shoes, which include many road running shoes, help absorb impact.

Some walking shoes utilize firmer midsole materials to emphasize stability and support over cushion. For example, the Birkenstock Arizona Soft Footbed sandals have a cork/latex midsole that is not plush, but rather molds over time to your footshape and is more supportive. Some of our testers have worn the Birks as walking shoes (with socks when it gets chilly!) while recovering from, or preventing, plantar fasciitis for this reason.

Lifestyle walking shoes tend to offer a balance of cushion and support. The Icebug Mosi RB9X is a good example of that. Others lean more toward plush cushioning, like the On Cloud 6.

Minimalist, zero-drop walking shoes, like the Lems Mesa Verde or Xero Prio Neo, tend to get out of your foot’s way in the sense that they’re more there to protect you from the elements (e.g., nails on a sidewalk) rather than to cushion or support your feet.

Upper material

The materials used in the uppers of walking shoes can vary greatly, from breathable mesh to hardy suede. Some have waterproof membranes, like the HOKA Clifton 11 GTX shoes, which we’d only recommend if you will frequently be walking and running in wet environments (otherwise, the waterproofness will limit breathability and your feet will get more sweaty).

Consider shoes with uppers that make sense for your activities and weather conditions. Shoes with mesh uppers will reduce how sweaty your feet get, whereas more durable upper materials, like suede, will hold up longer and in more rugged environments.

Breathability

Breathability may be a priority if you’ll be walking for long periods or in warm or humid environments. If that’s the case, consider walking shoes with mesh uppers that allow for more airflow, like road running shoes.

If you’ll be walking in wet or cold environments, you may want to prioritize waterproofness or insulation. Walking shoes with waterproof membranes will help keep your feet dry, though this means they won’t be as breathable.

Outsole grip

Outsole grip and traction might be an important factor when choosing walking shoes if you’ll often be walking on technical trails or slippery floors. Slip resistant shoes, like the HOKA Bondi SR and Arahi SR, provide extra grip on the sole, which can be particularly useful for restaurant/bar workers, nurses, and other jobs that involve slick floors. For example, HOKA adds a proprietary sticky compound to the outsole to make it stickier. The base is wider than running shoes too for more floor contact.

If most of your walking will be outside, particularly on gravel or more technical trails, you may want walking shoes with more traction. The HOKA Transport 2 shoes are a good example of walking shoes that have enough traction to transition to the trails.

Durability

Part of the Treeline Review ethos is buying gear that will last a long time, as this saves you money and helps limit the amount of gear that goes to landfills at the end of their life. Walking shoes made of durable rubber, suede, and other hardier materials will likely last a lot longer than shoes with more foam, like road running shoes. A great example of a long-lasting shoe is the Birkenstock Arizona Soft Footbed sandals, which are made of cork and suede/leather. Our testers have had pairs last nearly a decade of constant use.

Walking shoes that double as running shoes may not last as long as burlier walking shoes, but if the running shoes are the ones you’ll actually wear, those are the shoes you should get. Particularly when it comes to footwear, we recommend considering comfort and utility over durability.

Heel-to-toe drop and stability

Heel-to-toe drop (or offset) refers to the difference in stack height of the shoe from the heel to the toe. In general, people tend to heel-strike more while walking compared to running (meaning they land harder on their heel first and then roll into the rest of the foot). Folks who heel-strike may want a mid-drop or high-drop shoe for more impact absorption and to help guide the foot through a more natural gait.

Shoes with a higher heel-to-toe drop (7 mm or more) provide more impact absorption at the heel for those who heel strike. Zero-drop shoes, like the Birkenstock Arizona Soft Footbed Sandals, Lems Mesa Verde, and Xero Prio Neo, have the same stack height at the heel as they do at the toe (so, 0 mm), and allow you to walk with your natural gait. If you’re unsure which route to go, we typically recommend trying a mid-drop shoe (4-6 mm).

Sizing and width options

With most of the walking shoes we tested, you should be fine to go with your usual size. However, if you start spending a lot more time on your feet than you have before, it’s possible your feet will swell a little bit, in which case you may need a half-size or even full-size bigger. If you have wide feet, consider shoes available in wide sizing (shoes from brands like HOKA or barefoot shoe brands tend to accommodate wider feet).

We also recommend purchasing from retailers that offer free returns when possible, in case you find the fit isn’t right after a few uses.

Weight

The weight of a shoe is less important for walking than it is for activities like running or hiking, where you feel the impact of the shoe’s weight more over time. In general, walking shoes tend to be about the same or a little heavier than road running shoes, but lighter than hiking shoes.

Weight and packability, however, can be important if you are traveling and want to limit how many shoes you carry/pack.

Flexibility

While walking shoes should be stable enough to feel supportive, shoe flexibility can help keep your feet more comfortable over long periods of wear. Stiff walking shoes take more effort to walk in, and could cause fatigue faster if you’ll be wearing them all day.

Most shoes in this guide should be flexible enough for all-day comfort. If this is an important consideration for you though, the most flexible shoes we tested are the Xero Prio Neo barefoot shoes.

Versatility

You’ll be more likely to wear your walking shoes if they serve more than one purpose in your wardrobe. Consider all the use cases for your walking shoes besides walking, like running, gym workouts, hiking, traveling, commuting, work wear, recovery jogs, or casual meet-ups with friends.

Types of walking shoes explained

Athletic walking shoes vs. casual walking shoes

Athletic walking shoes are designed for active movement and functionality, while casual/lifestyle walking shoes are designed more for everyday walking (think errands, commuting, etc.) and style.

Athletic walking shoes, like road running shoes, tend to have cushioned midsoles that also focus on energy return and give you that sort of “bouncy” or “forward motion” sensation. They also have more breathable uppers for higher-output activities, and tend to offer more stability and support.

Casual/lifestyle walking shoes tend to be more minimally cushioned and without the energy return. They are focused less on functionality and more on style. You could walk and stand all day in casual walking shoes, but they wouldn’t be your best option for exercise beyond walking.

Motion control vs. stability vs. neutral shoes

Motion control, stability, and neutral shoes are designed to support different gaits, depending on your pronation. Pronation refers to the inward roll of the foot when stepping. Overpronation means the feet roll inward more than they should, and may indicate flat feet or collapsed arches. If you overpronate a lot, you may want to consider motion-control shoes.

Stability shoes are designed to help stabilize slight overpronation, while motion-control shoes stabilize the feet even more for more aggressive overpronation. The HOKA Arahi SR shoes are stability shoes. We did not test motion-control shoes for this guide, but running shoe brands like ASICS and New Balance offer excellent options.

Neutral shoes are the most common shoes on the market, and almost entirely what we’ve tested. They’re best for feet that have more neutral pronation. If your feet supinate, or roll outward, you’ll want to consider a pair of highly cushioned neutral shoes, like the HOKA Bondi SR, HOKA Bondi 9, or other max-cushioned road running shoes.

Another option is to choose neutral shoes and replace the manufacturer’s insert with custom orthotics. If you aren’t sure what type of shoe you need, we recommend seeing a podiatrist. Many running stores also often offer a gait analysis service and will point you in the direction of what shoe type will best suit you.

Slip-on vs. lace-up designs

Slip-on walking shoes allow you to simply slide your feet into the shoes without the need for adjusting them, whereas lace-up designs require tying and untying to secure and adjust the fit.

Slip-on shoes are great for everyday walking and casual wear, like running errands, commuting, or at work, since a more secure fit is usually less necessary in those cases. These are also a great option for folks with mobility constraints that might make putting on shoes difficult. For example, while the author was recovering from hip replacement surgery, slip-on shoe options, like the Birkenstock Arizona Soft Footbed sandals, the On Cloudaway 2, and the On Cloud 6, were the easiest shoes to wear.

Lace-up shoes, or shoes with toggles or other adjustments, allow for a more secure fit. This makes them a better choice for more active pursuits.

Waterproof vs. breathable materials

Most walking shoes are made of breathable materials, like mesh uppers, to promote airflow and prevent overheating or sweating (and thus, reduce odor and the risk of blisters). Opt for shoes with breathable materials if you’ll be active in your walking shoes, or if you’ll be wearing them for long periods (such as all day at work or long travel days).

Waterproof shoes have a membrane to keep water out, which makes them less breathable. You’ll only want waterproof walking shoes if most of your walking will be in wet conditions like wet commutes in Seattle or traveling to the UK.

Minimalist vs. maximum cushioning styles

Walking shoes come in a variety of cushion levels, from minimalist shoes, like the Xero Shoes Prio Neo, to maximalist shoes, like the HOKA Bondi SR. While we appreciate some cushion to keep our joints happy and help keep us comfortable throughout the day, the level of cushion is up to you and your comfort.

You may want to consider a more cushioned shoe if you’ll be standing on your feet all day, if walking is part of an active recovery routine after intense exercise, or if your feet supinate (roll outward).

Minimal-cushioned shoes are better for folks who prefer a more natural ground feel. This allows them to listen to their body and feel more grounded, agile, balanced, and in tune with their walking experience.

How to choose the right walking shoe size

Foot measurement

If you don’t already know your shoe size, you can do a simple at-home measurement by placing a socked foot on a piece of paper, tracing your foot, and measuring the length from heel to toe. You can also then measure the width at the widest part of your foot. Try doing this in the afternoon or after a lot of time on your feet, and trace while placing pressure on the ground, to account for any foot flattening.

Keep in mind that different shoes will feel different on your feet, and brand size accuracy varies. You’ll also want to consider arch support and foot volume when choosing your size as well. Ultimately, trying on shoes and wearing them around is the best way to determine proper fit and comfort.

When to try on shoes

As mentioned previously, something to consider while trying on walking shoes is that your feet may swell a little throughout the day, especially if you’re walking or standing a lot. Trying on shoes first thing in the morning versus after work or a workout will feel different. If you can, try on shoes later in the day or after a walk to ensure they’ll feel comfortable with some foot swelling. You also may want to opt for shoes that have laces or other adjustments if you typically spend very long hours on your feet.

Care and maintenance tips

It's important to read the manufacturer’s care instructions. Typically, you can wash most walking shoes in a washing machine on a delicate cycle with cold water and gentle detergent.

Some shoes can be put in the dryer on low or no heat, but some shoes can be damaged from the heat. In our opinion it is not worth the risk, and we usually air dry our shoes (and stuff newspaper inside to expedite the process). However, if the manufacturer instructions allow dryer use, we recommend keeping the heat low, tying the shoes together, and putting them in a mesh laundry bag to limit the potential for damage. We also recommend removing the insoles while drying to help them maintain their proper shape.

Store your dry walking shoes out of the sun if you can, and preferably somewhere they won’t be knocked around and damaged. Most shoes are pretty hardy, but this will help ensure they last longer.

Like with all apparel and gear, periodically check for wear. Common places for wear and damage include the upper material at the toe box and on the outsole tread. Worn-down midsoles are difficult to detect visually, but you can often feel the lack of cushion while wearing them (often in the form of new aches in your joints). When these elements of the shoe wear down, it’s time to replace them.

FAQ

What's the difference between walking shoes and running shoes?

While running shoes can be used as walking shoes, walking-specific shoes typically prioritize support, comfort, and style, whereas running shoes also prioritize breathability, reducing impact, traction, and propelling forward motion.

Road running shoes can make great walking shoes, as they tend to be comfortable, adequately supportive, and designed for the same surfaces people commonly walk on.

If most of your walking will be at a slower pace, or you prefer a more casual or trendy style, you may not want running shoes. In fact, road running shoes can encourage more forward motion than you’ll want for more casual movements, and can strain parts of your foot. Walking-specific shoes support slower movement, and provide cushion that’s engineered more for standing all day rather than impact absorption from pavement-pounding. With slip-on versions available, walking shoes also tend to come in more accessible options for those with mobility restrictions (or who simply prefer not having to tie their shoes).

How often should I replace my walking shoes?

This depends on factors like the shoe’s materials and construction quality, what surfaces you’re walking on, how often you wear them, how much weight or force they’re bearing, and more. But with proper care, most walking shoes should last about 300-500 miles. Depending on how often and how long you’re walking, that could be anywhere from a few months to a year or so.

However, some more durable shoes can last several years–many of our testers have known their Birkenstock Arizona Soft Footbed sandals to last nearly a decade of frequent use.

Signs of wear may first occur either in the upper (e.g., holes in the mesh near the toes), the outsole (e.g., the tread smoothing down and losing traction), or the midsole (your joints start to hurt more after wearing them). These are signs it’s time to replace your shoes.

Can I use walking shoes for hiking?

Some walking shoes can double as hiking shoes, especially on non-technical trails. Look for shoes made with durable materials and an outsole with lugs for traction. The HOKA Transport 2 are a good example of this, as they are durable, have a Vibram outsole, and are well suited for hiking. Those shoes are also available in a waterproof GTX version for waterproofness if you will be frequently hiking in wet conditions.For hiking shoe options, see our Best Hiking Shoes for Men guide.

Do I need special socks for walking shoes?

You do not need special socks for walking. However, we recommend socks that are comfortable and moisture wicking. After a quality pair of shoes, socks play a key role in foot comfort.

We recommend socks made from moisture-wicking materials, like synthetic blends (polyester/nylon), or merino or alpaca wool blends. Some folks prefer socks with a little cushion for added comfort. You can learn more in our Best Hiking Socks and Best Running Socks guides.

How do I know if I need motion-control walking shoes?

Motion-control walking shoes help correct significant overpronation (when your feet roll inward when you step, and you typically have collapsed arches). To determine if this is you, you can get a gait analysis from a professional or at a running store.

Motion-control shoes are designed to prevent your foot from rolling in. If your feet only overpronate a little, you can either opt for stability shoes or neutral shoes and use custom orthotics or inserts to help correct your gait.

Are expensive walking shoes worth it?

In general, a quality pair of walking shoes typically costs $130-160. You can find some that are more expensive but have more bells and whistles (e.g., the HOKA Bondi SR shoes cost $180 but offer slip-resistant grip and are more durable than running shoes). You’ll also pay more for waterproofness or premium materials (like leather). Decide which qualities matter most to you, what style you’ll actually wear, and how often you’ll wear the shoes.

You can always find walking shoes that are less expensive, especially on sale or clearance. You don’t have to spend a lot of money, just make sure they’re comfortable, supportive, and something you’ll actually wear.

Can walking shoes help with plantar fasciitis?

No shoe will cure plantar fasciitis, but properly supportive shoes can help you as you heal and follow your healthcare provider’s advice. Plantar fasciitis occurs when the band of tissue underfoot that connects your heel to your toes becomes inflamed. Walking can exacerbate the pain and inflammation, and we recommend seeing a healthcare professional for recovery advice. Most walkers find that supportive and comfortable shoes are more helpful as you heal than unsupportive and uncomfortable shoes.

The author had a bad case of plantar fasciitis years ago and learned she needed supportive, low- or mid-drop shoes that were comfortable without being too plush. In her case (and in the case of many), the Birkenstock Arizona Soft Footbed Sandals were the right cocktail of supportive and comfortable while she recovered.

What's the best way to break in new walking shoes?

The best way to break in new walking shoes is to first wear them on short walks and gradually increase either time on your feet or distance walked. All the walking shoes we tested—with the exception of zero-drop shoes if you are not already accustomed to them—are comfortable out of the box and do not require much, if any, break-in time. That said, shoes made with stiff materials (like leather), or shoes with a drastically different drop than you normally wear, should be eased into. A break-in period also allows you to see if any part of the shoe rubs uncomfortably, which could cause hot spots or blisters.

How we researched and tested

We tested these walking shoes over several years, walking hundreds of miles on paved paths, dirt and gravel trails, and hard floors at work. We tested while running errands, wandering around town, hiking, going for walks, working 7–12-hour shifts at breweries and hospitals, walk commuting, and traveling. Our testers wore these shoes across all seasons and weather conditions, ranging from hot, dry summer days to cold, drizzly rain storms, icy winter mornings, and everything in between.

Our testers each have different foot sizes and shapes, so we were able to test shoes with narrow, average, and wide feet.

Most testing surfaces were on paved paths or in indoor spaces, but we also tested every pair on gravel surfaces to assess grip and whether small rocks would wedge themselves in the outsole tread. Additionally, we tested them side by side with other walking shoes, running shoes, and recovery shoes.

